{"t":1790408373,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"cc1e6152-4e96-4a81-89f6-7cf516453848","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-26T01:12:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 260112\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n912 PM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nHave increased the sky cover forecast for along and east of I-95\ngiven current satellite trends. Additionally, with the westward\nshift in the precipitation forecast, measurable rainfall chances\nhave been raised for the second half of Saturday. For the tidal\nforecast, outside of Dahlgren, water levels have been a bit\nlower than anticipated. Tidal flooding does appear likely where\nCoastal Flood Advisories have been issued.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) A Nor'easter brings below normal temperatures, breezy\n  conditions, and some rain at times through this weekend.\n\n- 2) Warm and calm conditions next week before rainfall chances\n  return by late next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Nor'easter brings below normal temperatures,\nbreezy conditions, and some rain at times through this weekend.\n\nThe center of the circulation of the Nor'easter remains well off\nthe Delmarva coast this evening. Despite its offshore position,\nthe western edge of the high cirrus shield continues to pivot\nslowly toward the west in time. Aside from that, the atmospheric\nprofile is quite dry owing to the prevailing northerly winds in\nthe column. With the loss of daytime heating, most locations\nhave seen the breezy factor to the winds dropping off after\ndark. Tonight's lows will eventually fall into the 50s east of\nthe Blue Ridge, with mainly mid/upper 40s off to the west. The\none exception would be the Alleghenies which could fall into the\nlow 40s.\n\nGlobal models have come into much better agreement with respect\nto the track of the Nor'easter with rain becoming increasingly\nlikely Saturday afternoon into Sunday. Have confidence that the\nrain will reach as far west as the Blue Ridge Mountains and \neven portions of I-81 Saturday night. Quarter to half inch of \nrain expected for areas east of the Blue Ridge Mountains and an \ninch or more for areas east of I-95, particularly over northeast\nMaryland. Rainfall rates will be light to moderate since \ninstability will not be a concern. As for winds, wind gusts to \naround 30 mph are expected, perhaps as high as 35 mph along the \nwestern shore of the Chesapeake Bay. Nor'easter will begin to\nexit the area Sunday night with rain chances diminishing,\nalthough clouds will likely persist into Monday.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm and calm conditions next week before \nrainfall chances return by late next week.\n\nAs this weekend's Nor'easter slowly pushes offshore early next week, \ncloud cover and potential for showers diminish. High pressure builds \nin overhead briefly behind the departing Nor'easter bringing dry \nconditions to begin next week. A gradual warming trend is expected \nas highs go from low to mid 70s on Monday to around 80F Wednesday \nand Thursday. Mid-level ridging might develop across much of the \neastern CONUS at some point during the middle to late next week. \nThere's still a lot of variance in the model guidance, but if that \nwere to happen we could see highs reach well into the 80s at some \npoint. \n\nThe next best chance for rain looks to be at the end of next week \nwhen a stronger cold front approaches from the west.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nThe main impacts will be the gusty N or NNW winds up to 25 kt \nSaturday. Winds should start gradually diminishing Sat night \ninto Sunday, but still a little breezy. Rain is looking likely \nat all airports, except KCHO, starting Saturday afternoon and \ncontinuing into Sunday. \n\nOn Monday, VFR conditions are expected across all terminals through \nthe middle of next week. Northeasterly winds persist, but reduce in \nmagnitude sometime on Monday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nAs the Nor'easter churns offshore of the Delmarva coast, winds \nare expected to strengthen tonight through Saturday with gale \nforce winds likely south of Pooles Island.\n\nNortherly winds continue to weaken below SCA criteria on Monday and \ninto the middle of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nMinor coastal flooding will continue for one or two more high\ntide cycles for Anne Arundel, Calvert, and Alexandria, but as\nwinds strengthen from the N expect blowout tides through the\nweekend at those locations and locations further north on the\nChesapeake Bay. Minor coastal flooding will persist at Dahlgren\nand St. Marys County for several high tide cycles. As Nor'easter\ndeparts early next week, a snap back event may occur with \nrenewed minor coastal flooding possible.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EDT Saturday for MDZ008.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 8 AM EDT Monday for MDZ016.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 5 PM EDT Sunday for MDZ017.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 7 AM EDT Saturday for MDZ018.\nVA...Coastal Flood Advisory until 8 AM EDT Monday for VAZ057.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EDT Saturday for ANZ530-535-\n     536-538.\n     Gale Warning until 10 PM EDT Saturday for ANZ531-532-539>541.\n     Gale Warning until 6 PM EDT Saturday for ANZ533-534-537-542-\n     543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/LFR/BRO/KVH\nAVIATION...ADS/LFR/BRO/KVH\nMARINE...ADS/LFR/BRO/KVH\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...LFR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1790408360,"days":{"2026-09-26":{"hi":68,"lo":58,"pop":67,"short":"Light Rain Likely"},"2026-09-27":{"hi":64,"lo":60,"pop":64,"short":"Light Rain Likely"},"2026-09-28":{"hi":71,"lo":61,"pop":3,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-09-29":{"hi":75,"lo":60,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-30":{"hi":79,"lo":62,"pop":6,"short":"Mostly 